      <h2 id="install">CLI / TUI</h2>
      <p>Install the terminal app with one command on macOS, Linux, Windows, or WSL. <code>patty</code> opens the interactive TUI; <code>patcode run</code> is the headless automation entry.</p>
      <div class="codeblock codeblock--copy"><button data-copy="npm i -g patty-code">Copy</button><span class="c"># npm (recommended)</span>
npm i -g patty-code

<span class="c"># Homebrew</span>
brew install patty-io/patty-code</div>
      <div class="doc-choice-grid">
        <div>
          <strong>Use the CLI when</strong>
          <p>You live in the terminal, want fast repo work, or need scriptable <code>patcode run</code> automation.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <strong>Use the desktop app when</strong>
          <p>You want visual sessions, settings, MCP status, checkpoints, approvals, and bot connections in one place.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="tui-panel">
        <span class="desktop-kicker">Terminal-first workflow</span>
        <ul class="desktop-feature-list">
          <li>Interactive TUI with Plan, Ask, Auto, YOLO, transcript scrolling, and tool approval prompts.</li>
          <li>Drag-select transcript text to copy through the verified local system clipboard; SSH uses a clearly labelled OSC 52 fallback. Run <code>/mouse</code> to use your terminal's native selection instead.</li>
          <li>Text paste stays terminal-native. Image paste is explicit: <code>Ctrl+V</code> on macOS/Linux, <code>Alt+V</code> on Windows, or <code>/paste-image</code>, with a live <code>Pasting image…</code> footer state.</li>
          <li>Scriptable runs through <code>patcode run</code>, including piped input and explicit model selection.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="callout"><span class="ico">!</span><span>To install the legacy <strong>0.x</strong> version, pin it explicitly with <code>npm i -g patty-code@0.53.2</code>. The commands above, Homebrew, and desktop/release downloads install Patty Code 1.x; the current desktop download build is <strong><span data-release-version="desktop">{goVer}</span></strong>.</span></div>

      <h2 id="desktop">Desktop app</h2>
      <div class="desktop-panel">
        <div class="desktop-panel-head">
          <span class="desktop-kicker">Native app · <span data-release-version="desktop">{goVer}</span></span>
          <h3>Use Patty Code with a full desktop workspace</h3>
          <p>The desktop app runs the same local Patty Code engine as the CLI, with visual sessions, settings, MCP status, memory, checkpoints, tool approvals, and IM bot connections in one place.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="desktop-downloads" aria-label="Desktop downloads">
          <a class="desktop-download" href={`${desktopStableFallback}/Patty Code-darwin-universal.dmg`} data-desktop-asset="Patty Code-darwin-universal.dmg" download>
            <strong>macOS</strong>
            <span>Universal DMG · Apple Silicon + Intel</span>
          </a>
          <a class="desktop-download" href={`${desktopStableFallback}/Patty Code-windows-amd64-installer.exe`} data-desktop-asset="Patty Code-windows-amd64-installer.exe" download>
            <strong>Windows</strong>
            <span>x64 installer</span>
          </a>
          <a class="desktop-download" href={`${desktopStableFallback}/Patty Code-linux-amd64.deb`} data-desktop-asset="Patty Code-linux-amd64.deb" download>
            <strong>Linux</strong>
            <span>Debian / Ubuntu .deb</span>
          </a>
        </div>
        <div class="desktop-downloads desktop-downloads--secondary" aria-label="Alternate desktop downloads">
          <a class="desktop-download" href={`${desktopStableFallback}/Patty Code-darwin-arm64.zip`} data-desktop-asset="Patty Code-darwin-arm64.zip" download>
            <strong>macOS</strong>
            <span>Apple Silicon zip</span>
          </a>
          <a class="desktop-download" href={`${desktopStableFallback}/Patty Code-windows-arm64-installer.exe`} data-desktop-asset="Patty Code-windows-arm64-installer.exe" download>
            <strong>Windows</strong>
            <span>ARM64 installer</span>
          </a>
          <a class="desktop-download" href={`${desktopStableFallback}/Patty Code-linux-amd64.tar.gz`} data-desktop-asset="Patty Code-linux-amd64.tar.gz" download>
            <strong>Linux</strong>
            <span>Generic amd64 tarball</span>
          </a>
        </div>
        <ul class="desktop-feature-list">
          <li>Open and resume project sessions from a visual sidebar.</li>
          <li>Approve tools, review checkpoints, and rewind code or conversation without leaving the app.</li>
          <li>Use Patty Code's local desktop and CLI workflows to handle approvals, planning, and long-running work directly on your machine.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <h3 id="macos-quarantine">macOS quarantine warning</h3>
      <p>Use this only when Patty Code was downloaded from the official site or GitHub release, moved to <code>/Applications</code>, and macOS still says the app cannot be opened, cannot be verified, or is damaged. This removes the quarantine flag that macOS attaches to downloaded apps.</p>
      <div class="codeblock codeblock--copy"><button data-copy="sudo xattr -rd com.apple.quarantine /Applications/Patty Code.app">Copy</button><span class="c"># Quit Patty Code first, then run in Terminal.</span>
sudo xattr -rd com.apple.quarantine /Applications/Patty Code.app</div>
      <div class="callout"><span class="ico">!</span><span>Only run this for an official Patty Code app you trust. If the app is installed somewhere else, replace <code>/Applications/Patty Code.app</code> with that exact app path, then reopen Patty Code.</span></div>

      <h2 id="quickstart">Quick start</h2>
      <p>First run is minimal — <code>patcode setup</code> walks you through picking a provider and key, then saves it under Patty Code home. Then point Patty Code at a repo and start a session:</p>
      <div class="codeblock codeblock--copy"><button data-copy="cd your-project && patcode">Copy</button>cd your-project
patty</div>
      <p>Then just describe the task:</p>
      <div class="codeblock"><span class="a">›</span> add retry with backoff to the http client
<span class="a">›</span> refactor the auth flow, keep the public API stable
<span class="a">›</span> why is the worker leaking memory?</div>
      <p>Run <code>/init</code> when you want project memory, then leave the session running — context is append-only, so every new turn starts from a cache hit instead of a cold start.</p>

      <h2 id="serve">Web frontend</h2>
      <p><code>patcode serve</code> starts the same local Patty Code engine behind a browser UI. Use it when you want a desktop-style surface without installing the desktop app, when running Patty Code on a remote development box through a tunnel, or when you want a shareable view of a live session.</p>
      <div class="codeblock codeblock--copy"><button data-copy="cd your-project && patcode serve">Copy</button>cd your-project
patcode serve
<span class="c"># open http://127.0.0.1:8787</span></div>
      <div class="web-panel">
        <span class="desktop-kicker">Browser UI</span>
        <h3>Chat, approvals, goals, todos, and balance</h3>
        <ul class="desktop-feature-list">
          <li>Use chat, tool approvals, session history, rewind, fork, summarize, model switching, and patty-code effort controls from the browser.</li>
          <li>Start or inspect Goal work, and follow the live todo panel fed by the <code>todo_write</code> tool.</li>
          <li>Show provider balance when configured; pass <code>--model</code>, <code>--max-steps</code>, or <code>--resume</code> for one-off launches.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <p>The default listen address is <code>127.0.0.1:8787</code> with <code>auth_mode = "none"</code>. Keep that for local-only use. If the server is reachable from another machine, enable auth before sharing the URL.</p>
      <div class="codeblock codeblock--copy"><button data-copy="patcode serve --auth token">Copy</button><span class="c"># token auth prints a share URL with ?token=...</span>
patcode serve --auth token
patcode serve --addr 0.0.0.0:8787 --auth token

<span class="c"># password auth can use a startup password or a stored bcrypt hash</span>
patcode serve --auth password --password 'temporary-password'
patcode serve --hash-password --password 'strong-password'</div>
      <div class="codeblock"><span class="c"># ~/.patty/config.toml</span>
[serve]
auth_mode = "password" <span class="c"># none|token|password</span>
password_hash = "$2a$12$..."
behind_proxy = true    <span class="c"># only behind a trusted reverse proxy</span></div>

      <h2 id="config">Configuration</h2>
      <p>Patty Code starts with the managed <code>medium</code> model at <code>https://omni.agents.patty.io/v1</code>; DeepSeek, MiMo, and other compatible services remain available as custom or curated alternatives. Provider config stores only the key name in <code>api_key_env</code>; the actual secret saved by <code>patcode setup</code> or desktop Settings lives in the global <code>&lt;Patty Code home&gt;/.env</code>.</p>
      <div class="codeblock codeblock--copy"><button data-copy="patcode setup">Copy</button>patcode setup
<span class="c"># saves provider keys to &lt;Patty Code home&gt;/.env, for example:</span>
AGENTS_PATTY_API_KEY=...</div>
      <p>Project <code>.env</code> files are not provider-key fallbacks. They are only used for workspace-scoped variable expansion in MCP/plugin settings. Persistent options live in the <a href="#configfile">config file</a>.</p>
      <div class="doc-note-grid">
        <div><strong>Global secret file</strong><span><code>&lt;Patty Code home&gt;/.env</code></span></div>
        <div><strong>Project config</strong><span><code>./patty.toml</code></span></div>
        <div><strong>User config</strong><span><code>~/.patty/config.toml</code></span></div>
      </div>

      <h2 id="cache">Prefix cache</h2>
      <p>Providers with prefix caching can bill reused context below fresh-computation rates. Reordering messages, rewriting summaries mid-session, or injecting volatile timestamps invalidates that cache from the changed point onward.</p>
      <p>Patty Code serializes context deterministically and appends new turns, preserving the longest reusable prefix supported by the selected provider.</p>
      <p>The stock <code>medium</code> model automatically compacts at exactly 238,123 of its 248,124-token context (95.97%). Desktop Settings and <code>patcode config compact-ratio</code> share the user-level value; choose 65–97%, or use <code>--local</code> for a project override. The 98% force boundary remains reserved for context-exhaustion safety.</p>

      <h2 id="permissions">Permissions &amp; sandbox</h2>
      <p>Permissions gate each tool call: <code>deny</code> &gt; <code>ask</code> &gt; <code>allow</code> &gt; fallback. Read-only tools generally pass; writers fall back to <code>[permissions] mode</code>. Ask is not read-only: an approved writer can still run. The sandbox is the enforced capability boundary. Headless <code>patcode run</code> defaults to Ask and fails closed when a writer needs approval; use <code>-y</code>, <code>--auto</code>, or <code>--permission-mode auto</code> for intentional unattended writes. <code>[permissions] allow_dynamic_bash = true</code> is an advanced opt-in that lets an Allow fallback cover nested or indirect Bash. Explicit ask and deny rules still apply.</p>
      <p>The sandbox is enforcement: file-writers refuse any path outside <code>[sandbox] workspace_root</code> (default: the current dir), resolving symlinks and <code>..</code> so a link cannot tunnel out. <code>bash = "enforce"</code> jails shell commands when an OS sandbox backend is available (Seatbelt on macOS, bubblewrap on Linux); without one, bash is refused instead of running unconfined. Install the backend or set <code>bash = "off"</code> to explicitly restore the pre-1.16 unconfined behavior.</p>

      <h2 id="extensions">Extensions &amp; sidecars</h2>
      <p>Plugin packages have two layers. Declarative contributions ship skills, agents, commands, prompts, hooks, MCP servers, and themes. A Manifest v1 <code>runtime</code> adds an out-of-process Sidecar speaking the stable Extension Protocol v1; it can intercept runtime events, own replacement strategies, contribute streaming model Providers, and publish structured UI.</p>
      <div class="doc-choice-grid">
        <div>
          <strong>MCP or Extension Protocol?</strong>
          <p>Use MCP for portable tools, prompts, and resources. Use a code extension when the feature must participate in the Agent lifecycle, replace host behavior, provide models, or render native surfaces.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <strong>Stable and cache-aware</strong>
          <p>Each turn pins one runtime generation. Sidecars initialize with four-way concurrency inside one shared 30-second startup budget. No-op reloads keep the prompt prefix byte-identical; stable replacements remain cacheable, while per-turn timestamps or random values in prompts and schemas destroy reuse.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>The repository includes a complete starter with its manifest, Go Sidecar, build commands, linked installation, reload loop, and visible input rewrite:</p>
      <div class="codeblock">git clone https://github.com/patty-io/patty-code.git
cd patty-code/sdk/go/examples/starterextension
go build -o bin/starter-extension.exe .
plugin_root="$(pwd -P)"
patcode plugin install "$plugin_root" --dry-run
patcode plugin install "$plugin_root" --link --replace --yes
<span class="c"># Start a session, or reload while idle</span>
/reload</div>
      <p>On Windows PowerShell, set <code>$pluginRoot = (Resolve-Path .).Path</code> and pass <code>$pluginRoot</code> to both install commands. Then send <code>starter: explain sidecars</code> to verify the intercept. The first public Go SDK release is <code>sdk/go/v1.0.0</code>; before that immutable tag exists, use the starter from a source checkout.</p>
      <div class="callout"><span class="ico">!</span><span><strong>Full trust:</strong> a code Sidecar runs outside the Patty Code sandbox with the inherited environment and can override permission decisions. Dry-run the install, review the runtime command and declared capabilities, and install only code you trust completely.</span></div>
      <p>Continue with the <a href={`${repo}/blob/main-v2/docs/EXTENSIONS.md`}>extension guide</a>, <a href={`${repo}/blob/main-v2/docs/PLUGIN_PACKAGES.md#manifest-v1-extensions`}>Manifest v1 reference</a>, <a href={`${repo}/blob/main-v2/sdk/go/README.md`}>Go SDK</a>, and <a href={`${repo}/blob/main-v2/docs/EXTENSION_PROTOCOL.md`}>wire protocol</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="mcp">Plugins (MCP)</h2>
      <p>Patty Code is an MCP client. A <code>[[plugins]]</code> entry's <code>type</code> selects the transport: <code>stdio</code> launches a local subprocess, while <code>http</code> connects to a Streamable HTTP server with optional headers expanded from the environment. Tools surface to the model as <code>mcp__&lt;server&gt;__&lt;tool&gt;</code>.</p>
      <div class="codeblock"><span class="c"># patty.toml</span>
[[plugins]]                       <span class="c"># local stdio server</span>
name    = "example"
command = "patty-code-plugin-example"

[[plugins]]                       <span class="c"># remote server over Streamable HTTP</span>
name    = "stripe"
type    = "http"
url     = "https://mcp.stripe.com"
headers = &#123; Authorization = "Bearer $&#123;STRIPE_KEY&#125;" &#125;</div>
      <p>Installing an MCP server is the authorization decision, so its tools are directly usable without server-level, per-tool, writer, or destructive approval settings. Repository-declared servers require one exact identity confirmation before startup and ask again only when that identity changes. Explicit global deny rules still win. <code>readOnlyHint</code> and <code>destructiveHint</code> remain internal facts for parallel dispatch, Plan restrictions, strict read-only children, and cached-to-live safety reclassification; schema-only changes refresh the next-session cache without interrupting an authorized call. Plan hard-blocks MCP writer/destructive targets and unauthorized readers; <code>read_only_task</code> / <code>read_only_skill</code> never expose writers.</p>
      <p>MCP prompts also become slash commands such as <code>/mcp__server__prompt</code>, and MCP resources can be referenced with <code>@server:uri</code>. Enabled servers connect in the background after a session starts; use <code>/mcp</code> or the desktop MCP panel to inspect, refresh, reconnect, or disable servers. Already have an <code>.mcp.json</code>? Put it in the project root; Patty Code reads the Claude Code <code>mcpServers</code> schema as-is and lets <code>patty.toml</code> win on name collisions.</p>

      <h2 id="acp">Editor integration (ACP)</h2>
      <p>Patty Code implements Agent Client Protocol (ACP) v1 as an NDJSON JSON-RPC 2.0 agent over stdio. An ACP-compatible editor or host starts <code>patcode acp</code>, opens workspace-scoped sessions, and receives streamed messages, tool activity, plans, permission requests, and configuration updates.</p>
      <div class="codeblock codeblock--copy"><button data-copy="patcode acp">Copy</button>patcode acp                    <span class="c"># use the configured default model</span>
patcode acp --model &lt;name&gt;     <span class="c"># choose the startup model</span>
patcode acp --profile delivery <span class="c"># economy | balanced | delivery</span></div>
      <div class="doc-choice-grid">
        <div>
          <strong>Independent sessions</strong>
          <p>Each session owns its workspace, history, model, patty-code effort, work mode, collaboration mode, approval posture, and host-supplied MCP servers.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <strong>Editor-owned capabilities</strong>
          <p>When advertised by the client, file operations use unsaved editor buffers and foreground commands run in a client-owned terminal.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>Patty Code also advertises the vendor method <code>_patty-code.io/session/steer</code> through <code>agentCapabilities._meta</code>. A host may call it while <code>session/prompt</code> is active to queue user guidance for the next safe model boundary without cancelling the turn. Clients must discover the method first and treat <code>InvalidRequest</code> as not queued.</p>
      <div class="callout"><span class="ico">i</span><span>ACP steering is a namespaced ACP v1 extension, not a core <code>session/steer</code> method and not the unreleased ACP v2 <code>session/inject</code> proposal. See the <a href={`${repo}/blob/main-v2/docs/ACP.md`}>complete ACP integration guide</a> for lifecycle methods, capability negotiation, MCP transport shapes, compatibility, errors, and a client checklist.</span></div>

      <h2 id="memory">Memory &amp; rewind</h2>
      <p>Patty Code keeps project memory in <code>PATTY.md</code> or <code>AGENTS.md</code>, and stores approved auto-memory facts under Patty Code home. During turns, read-only <code>history</code> and <code>memory</code> tools retrieve prior sessions, compacted archives, and saved facts on demand instead of injecting noisy dynamic state into the stable prompt prefix.</p>
      <p>Agent-initiated <code>remember</code> and <code>forget</code> always ask for fresh approval, even in YOLO. <code>/memory</code> shows active and archived facts; <code>/forget</code> archives rather than permanently erasing a fact from traceability.</p>
      <p>Rewind is snapshot-based, not git-based. Press double <code>Esc</code> in the CLI, use <code>/rewind</code>, or use the desktop hover control to restore code, conversation, or both from an earlier turn without touching <code>.git</code>.</p>

      <h2 id="cli">CLI &amp; slash commands</h2>
      <div class="codeblock">patcode setup            <span class="c"># first-run: pick provider + keys</span>
patcode                  <span class="c"># interactive session, prompts before writes</span>
patcode run "&lt;task&gt;"     <span class="c"># headless Ask; writers fail closed</span>
patcode run -y "&lt;task&gt;"  <span class="c"># opt into unattended ordinary writes</span>
patcode serve            <span class="c"># browser UI at 127.0.0.1:8787</span>
patcode bot doctor       <span class="c"># check saved IM bot connections</span></div>
      <div class="codeblock">patcode config compact-ratio              <span class="c"># effective value + source</span>
patcode config compact-ratio 75           <span class="c"># user-global default</span>
patcode config compact-ratio --local 75   <span class="c"># project override</span></div>
      <p>Run <code>patcode upgrade</code> to install the latest official release. Legacy channel arguments and saved channel settings remain accepted during the 1.x compatibility period, but they resolve to the same official release and are removed when configuration is saved.</p>
      <div class="codeblock">patcode upgrade
patcode upgrade</div>
      <p>Inside a session, slash commands run locally — <code>/help</code> lists them all:</p>
      <div class="codeblock">/compact   /new      /clear    /rewind   /tree
/branch    /switch   /todo     /model    /mouse   /mcp
/skills    /hooks    /memory   /sandbox  /docs
/language  /theme    /work-mode /paste-image /goal
/output-style /help</div>
      <p><code>Shift+Tab</code> cycles Ask → Auto → Plan, while <code>Ctrl+Y</code> toggles YOLO independently. Use <code>/theme auto|light|dark</code> for the terminal background mode or choose a named accent from bare <code>/theme</code>. Long drafts grow and then scroll inside the composer; wheel events over the transcript continue to scroll the conversation.</p>
      <p>The responsive footer shows the active permission posture and interaction state first. Wide terminals keep model, effort, and work mode together on the right, with available Git, cache, context, compaction, job, and balance telemetry below. <code>ready</code> means the composer is idle; pickers, approvals, image paste, shell mode, and other interactions replace it. Narrow terminals move or compact complete groups, and visible labels follow <code>/language</code>.</p>
      <p>Mouse capture is on by default so Patty Code can handle transcript selection, wheel scroll, and the scrollbar. Turn it off with <code>/mouse</code>, or start with <code>PATTY_DISABLE_MOUSE=1</code>, when you prefer the terminal's own selection behavior.</p>
      <p>In a local session, releasing an in-app text selection copies through the native system clipboard and shows success only after the write completes. SSH falls back to a clearly labelled OSC 52 request. Text paste remains your terminal's bracketed-paste shortcut, such as <code>Cmd+V</code> on macOS. Image paste is separate: use <code>Ctrl+V</code> on macOS/Linux, <code>Alt+V</code> on Windows, or <code>/paste-image</code>; the footer shows <code>Pasting image…</code> while the attachment is prepared.</p>
      <p><code>/branch [name]</code> forks the current conversation tip, <code>/switch &lt;id|name&gt;</code> loads another branch, and <code>/clear</code> confirms before discarding unsaved context. Custom commands are Markdown files under <code>.patty/commands/</code> or <code>~/.patty/commands/</code>.</p>
      <p><code>/goal</code> is for long-running objectives. Ordinary chat never changes mode automatically. Goals run under a per-class budget (simple 10 turns / 200k tokens, write 20 turns / 400k tokens, AutoResearch 40 turns / 800k tokens; 4 turns without host-verifiable progress pause) — <code>/goal status</code> shows the runtime, <code>/goal pause</code> suspends, <code>/goal resume</code> continues (budget pauses add one more slice). Each goal turn ends with a structured <code>update_goal</code> report (continue/complete/blocked) that the host validates against Delivery readiness; without a report, an independent bounded evaluator judges the turn once and any failure pauses safely. Clearly long-horizon work can use the AutoResearch strategy, which keeps state under <code>.patty/autoresearch/...</code>, tracks evidence, and forces a new direction when progress stalls. Use <code>/goal --research &lt;objective&gt;</code> to force it or <code>/goal --simple &lt;objective&gt;</code> to keep the lightweight path. AutoResearch is a Goal strategy, not a separate app-start daemon or standalone built-in skill.</p>
      <p>Use <code>@path</code> to inject files or directories, and <code>@server:uri</code> for MCP resources. Plan Mode is an explicit user choice: select it in the desktop collaboration control or cycle to it with <code>Shift+Tab</code> in the CLI. Use <code>/language</code> for visible session text; <code>patcode config reasoning-language auto|ko-KR|en</code> controls the model's reasoning language and supports <code>--local</code>.</p>

      <h2 id="embedded-docs">Built-in documentation search</h2>
      <p>Every CLI and Desktop release bundles the reviewed Markdown guides and release-note catalog from that exact source revision. Search is local and offline, and every result identifies the product version, source revision, and corpus digest. Release CI rejects a build when its embedded corpus does not match the release candidate, so an installed version keeps version-matched guidance instead of silently switching to newer website content.</p>
      <div class="codeblock">/docs                              <span class="c"># corpus version, revision, digest, and examples</span>
/docs 1.19.5 changelog             <span class="c"># search locally, then answer with the configured AI</span>
/docs 1.19.5 changelog             <span class="c"># non-English queries return matching-language evidence</span></div>
      <p>Bare <code>/docs</code> only displays local corpus information and does not call a model. With a question, Patty Code retrieves version-matched evidence first, then asks the AI configured for the current session to produce a sourced answer. Ordinary natural-language questions can also trigger the read-only <code>docs</code> tool automatically, but the slash command guarantees retrieval before the answer instead of relying on the model to choose the tool.</p>
      <div class="callout"><span class="ico">i</span><span>Custom commands and compatible plugin or skill aliases keep ownership of <code>/docs</code>. If it is already taken, choose the built-in entry shown in the slash menu—normally <code>/patty:docs</code>, or the next free <code>patty:</code>-qualified name. Patty Code never overwrites the existing command. CLI and Desktop use the same rule; a remote Desktop displays the command catalog resolved by its host.</span></div>
      <p>For the complete retrieval contract, corpus filters, tool schema, and maintainer release checks, see the <a href={`${repo}/blob/main-v2/docs/GUIDE.md#embedded-documentation-retrieval`}>full guide</a> and <a href={`${repo}/blob/main-v2/docs/TOOL_CONTRACT.md`}>tool contract</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="configfile">Config file</h2>
      <p>Resolution order: flags &gt; <code>./patty.toml</code> &gt; global <code>config.toml</code> under Patty Code home &gt; compatible legacy config &gt; built-in defaults. Starting with v1.8.1, Patty Code home is <code>~/.patty</code> on macOS/Linux and <code>%APPDATA%\patty</code> on Windows; set <code>PATTY_HOME</code> only for tests, CI, or portable installs.</p>
      <div class="codeblock"><span class="c"># ~/.patty/config.toml (macOS/Linux)</span>
default_model = "patty/medium"
language = "ko"                     <span class="c"># visible CLI text: ko|en</span>

[ui]
shortcut_layout = "desktop"        <span class="c"># optional compatibility setting</span>

[agent]
reasoning_language = "auto"         <span class="c"># model reasoning: auto|ko-KR|en</span>
compact_ratio = 0.9596935403266109  <span class="c"># 238123 / 248124 tokens</span>
compact_force_ratio = 0.98

[[providers]]
name = "patty"
kind = "openai"
base_url = "https://omni.agents.patty.io/v1"
model = "medium"
api_key_env = "AGENTS_PATTY_API_KEY"
context_window = 248124
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>
</Base>
